Scientific Specialist

{"verdict_signal": "caution", "concerns": ["The genetic arms are respectable in structure (CRISPR KO plus two non-overlapping siRNAs, scrambled and vehicle controls, WT rescue) but the WT rescue alone cannot separate on-target catalytic/structural function from a scaffolding or complex-stability role — NSMCE3 is an obligate SMC5/6 complex subunit, so loss of it destabilises NSMCE2/SMC5/SMC6 and the phenotype may be a complex-collapse phenotype rather than a druggable NSMCE3 activity. That is exactly the off-pathway consequence the hub is asking about, and the catalytically-dead rescue is the arm that would resolve it. Its absence is the single largest gap.", "The unmeasured paralogue is not a minor loose end: if the paralogue is partially redundant, the KO/siRNA phenotype could reflect combined pathway loss, and if it is not redundant, the 12-fold selectivity of the tool compound becomes the interpretive bottleneck. Either way, no arm currently distinguishes NSMCE3-specific loss from SMC5/6 pathway loss.", "Concordance between genetic and pharmacological arms has not been demonstrated. An 800 nM, unoptimised, 12-fold-selective phenol-containing tool compound recapitulating the KO phenotype would be the cheapest independent line of evidence available, and it appears not to have been run head-to-head against the genetic arms in the same assay.", "n=6 per arm with no pre-specified analysis plan means the effect size is unconstrained and the p-values are not interpretable at face value. For a cell-based phenotype this may be adequate if the effect is large and the variance small, but nothing in the description lets the panel judge that. Post hoc analysis on n=6 is where irreproducible effects live.", "No independent replication. One lab, one time, own hands, own reagents. The Utrecht reagent request is an opportunity, not evidence.", "Two primary human explants is a very thin translational base, and 'within about 5-fold' between mouse and human curves is a comparison of potency, not of mechanism — it does not establish that the same pathway dependency operates in both. Recurrent GBM adds a specific worry: SMC5/6 and DNA-damage-response dependencies in glioma are strongly modulated by MGMT status, TP53 status and prior alkylator exposure, none of which is characterised in the explants. A dependency measured in treatment-naive tissue may not survive in lomustine-pretreated recurrent disease.", "Interpretability of the proposed next step is unstated. The PI describes making the catalytically-dead construct but has not framed what a negative result would mean or whether the programme would stop. As described, the work can confirm but has no defined disconfirming outcome."], "questions_to_ask": ["If NSMCE3 is an obligate subunit of SMC5/6, how do you know you are looking at loss of an NSMCE3 activity rather than collapse of the whole complex? Have you blotted SMC5, SMC6 and NSMCE2 in your KO and siRNA lines to see whether the rest of the complex is still there?", "When the catalytically-dead rescue arrives, what result would make you stop? Say the dead construct rescues as well as wild-type — is that the end of the target, or do you have an alternative interpretation ready?", "You have a tool compound at 800 nM. Have you run it side by side with the KO in the same assay and on the same plate — does the compound phenotype look like the genetic phenotype, and is the compound inert in the KO background? That epistasis experiment is cheap and would tell us a great deal.", "What is the paralogue, and what happens if you knock it down in your KO line — do you get more phenotype, or nothing? And is the paralogue expressed at all in your two human explants?", "For the n=6 experiments, what were the actual effect sizes and within-group variances, and was the primary readout chosen before or after you saw the data?", "Your two human explants — what were their MGMT methylation and TP53 status, and had either patient been treated with an alkylator before resection? Recurrent GBM is a pretreated, DNA-repair-selected population and I want to know whether the dependency you measured is likely to survive that.", "What exactly are you sending Utrecht, and would you be willing to have them run a pre-agreed replication of the primary phenotype with a blinded readout rather than an open-ended collaboration?"], "confidence": "moderate"}

