Clinical Specialist

{"verdict_signal": "caution", "concerns": ["The question as posed is partly outside my domain — 'preclinical ranking' is a rigor/methodology framing. What I can speak to is whether the clinical decision point is real: in recurrent IDH-wildtype GBM, developers do not lack candidates, they lack candidates that reach tumour. Preclinical potency ranking has historically failed to predict clinical outcome in this indication because CNS exposure, not target engagement in a dish, is the discriminating variable. Nothing stated addresses brain penetration, free-drug CSF/tumour concentration, or P-gp/BCRP efflux liability — so the preclinical package as described does not yet rank on the axis that actually decides GBM programmes.", "The 7-9 month lomustine benchmark is real but is a weak control by modern standards and increasingly contested — lomustine monotherapy is no longer uniformly the practical comparator, and regulators have pushed back on single-arm or lomustine-controlled designs where TTF-200 fields (tumour-treating fields), re-irradiation, or investigator's-choice arms are locally standard. 'Same endpoint three prior programmes used' is accurate but those programmes largely failed, which is precedent for the design, not for the bar being low.", "12,000/year US recurrent incidence is directionally right for the whole recurrent GBM population, but the enrolable slice is far smaller — good performance status, first recurrence, measurable disease, off high-dose steroids. Realistic OS-powered trials in this space have needed 400-700 patients across many sites and 3-4 years, which is a long, high-attrition path with no validated interim surrogate (PFS and response are unreliable in GBM because of pseudoprogression).", "No biomarker-selected population is proposed. The mechanism is described as target-driven and there is a 14-explant human dataset, but no stated stratifier (NSMCE3 expression, SMC5/6 complex status, a synthetic-lethal partner such as an HR-deficiency or replication-stress signature) that would define a responder slice. Without one, this is an all-comers OS trial — the highest-cost, lowest-information path — and no accelerated-approval mechanism is available.", "Standard-of-care drift risk over an 18-month-plus-4M-seed horizon is non-trivial: the recurrent GBM comparator landscape is moving (ONC201/dordaviprone in H3K27M-altered glioma has already reached approval in a defined subgroup, and multiple bispecific/CAR-T and ADC programmes are in registrational or near-registrational trials). A programme that enters the clinic in 3-4 years against an all-comers design may be comparing against a fragmented, biomarker-stratified field rather than lomustine."], "questions_to_ask": ["What is BLK-2117's free-drug concentration in brain tumour tissue and in normal brain relative to plasma, and is it a P-gp or BCRP substrate? In GBM the compounds that fail are almost always the ones that never got there \u2014 I'd want to see unbound brain-to-plasma ratio and intracranial efficacy in an orthotopic model, not flank.", "If you could only enrol 150 patients rather than 600, who would they be? Is there any NSMCE3-linked stratifier \u2014 expression level, SMC5/6 complex status, a replication-stress or HR-deficiency signature \u2014 that separated responders from non-responders across your 14 human explants?", "Your KOLs support the design \u2014 did either of them tell you what they'd actually randomise their own recurrent patients to today at their centre? I want to know whether lomustine is genuinely the comparator they'd accept, or whether you'd be arguing with regulators about an investigator's-choice or re-irradiation arm.", "Is there a de-risking indication before GBM \u2014 a systemic tumour with the same NSMCE3 dependency where you get a response-rate readout in 40 patients in 18 months instead of an OS readout in 600 patients over four years? Establishing proof-of-mechanism outside the CNS first would tell you whether the biology or the blood-brain barrier is the risk.", "What does your first-in-human design look like, and does it include paired tumour biopsy or a CSF pharmacodynamic marker at surgery \u2014 a window-of-opportunity cohort in patients going to resection \u2014 so you learn whether you engaged the target in human brain before committing to a randomised OS study?"], "confidence": "moderate"}

